// Test 25: A divide squashed by a taken fused branch
// klaussCPU 9ad6614 fixed this; it is the hardware session's board test.
// A taken FBR resolves in MEM and squashes the instruction behind it, which
// is then in EX. If that instruction is a DIVR that has already started the
// divider (MD_IDLE -> MD_PREP on the same edge), cores before 9ad6614 left
// the divider running on the dead operands, and the next DIVR within about
// 64 cycles got their quotient: here 1000 / 7 = 0x8E in place of
// 100 / 3 = 0x21. 9ad6614 resets the divider on a taken FBR (and on an EX
// squash), so the DIVR at the target gets its own quotient.
//
// Keep the FBR directly followed by the wrong-path DIVR, and the second DIVR
// at the target soon after; corpus_tests.rs checks that shape, because the
// emulator does not model the pipeline and passes either way. Three passes,
// the later ones from a warm I-cache.
//
// Expected UART output:
//   00000021  (100 / 3, pass 1)
//   00000021  (pass 2)
//   00000021  (pass 3)

_start
SETR P 0xFFFFF
COPY O P
SETR A 1
SETR B 1
SETR C 1000
SETR D 7
SETR E 100
SETR F 3
SETR J 3
AGAIN:
SETR G 0x55
SETR H 0x55
NOP
FBR A B 0 TGT:      // A == B: taken, resolves in MEM
DIVR G C D          // wrong path: in EX when the branch redirects
NOP
NOP
TGT:
DIVR H E F          // 100 / 3 = 0x21
$TXR H
$NEWLINE
DECR J
JMPNZ AGAIN:
HALT

!include uart_stubs.kla
